ML_PAGE("t1-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 1 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Topic 1 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l1\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t1-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t1-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"t1n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">28 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. Start with the deepest idea: risk only makes sense relative to an objective\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s1\" title=\"1. Start with the deepest idea: risk only makes sense relative to an objective\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Risk is relative to an objective</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Risk does not necessarily mean something bad\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s2\" title=\"2. Risk does not necessarily mean something bad\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Risk does not necessarily mean something bad</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Risk vs uncertainty - understand the information problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s3\" title=\"3. Risk vs uncertainty - understand the information problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Risk vs uncertainty</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Loss exposure: don&#x27;t confuse the exposure with the actual loss\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s4\" title=\"4. Loss exposure: don&#x27;t confuse the exposure with the actual loss\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Loss exposure vs actual loss</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Frequency and severity: the two dimensions behind most risk decisions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s5\" title=\"5. Frequency and severity: the two dimensions behind most risk decisions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Frequency and severity</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Risk identification: management cannot manage something it has not recognised\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s6\" title=\"6. Risk identification: management cannot manage something it has not recognised\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Risk identification methods</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Risk treatment: avoidance, prevention and reduction are NOT the same thing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s7\" title=\"7. Risk treatment: avoidance, prevention and reduction are NOT the same thing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Avoidance vs prevention vs reduction</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Now the difficult part: &quot;types of risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s8\" title=\"8. Now the difficult part: &quot;types of risk&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Types of risk: the overview</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Pure vs speculative risk asks: &quot;What possible outcomes exist?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s9\" title=\"9. Pure vs speculative risk asks: &quot;What possible outcomes exist?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Pure vs speculative risk</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable asks an entirely different question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s10\" title=\"10. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable asks an entirely different question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Why diversification works\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s11\" title=\"11. Why diversification works\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Why diversification works</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Non-diversifiable/systematic risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s12\" title=\"12. Non-diversifiable/systematic risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Non-diversifiable/systematic risk</button></li><li data-full=\"13. There is a terminology issue in your slides you should know about\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s13\" title=\"13. There is a terminology issue in your slides you should know about\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Systematic vs systemic risk</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Beta: why investors are compensated for some risks but not others\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s14\" title=\"14. Beta: why investors are compensated for some risks but not others\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Beta and compensated risk</button></li><li data-full=\"15. A very important higher-order insight: the classifications can overlap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s15\" title=\"15. A very important higher-order insight: the classifications can overlap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Classifications overlap</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Enterprise risk: now move from investment theory to the organisation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s16\" title=\"16. Enterprise risk: now move from investment theory to the organisation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Enterprise risk and risk cascades</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Operational risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s17\" title=\"17. Operational risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Operational risk</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Liquidity risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s18\" title=\"18. Liquidity risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Liquidity risk</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Credit risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s19\" title=\"19. Credit risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Credit risk</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Market risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s20\" title=\"20. Market risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Market risk</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Business risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s21\" title=\"21. Business risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Business risk</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Reputational risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s22\" title=\"22. Reputational risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Reputational risk</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Resource risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s23\" title=\"23. Resource risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Resource risk</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Now analyse the KFC Malaysia example on page 37\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s24\" title=\"24. Now analyse the KFC Malaysia example on page 37\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>KFC Malaysia case</button></li><li data-full=\"25. The chapter as one integrated system\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s25\" title=\"25. The chapter as one integrated system\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>The chapter as one integrated system</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy: how I would expect you to be tested\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s26\" title=\"26. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy: how I would expect you to be tested\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Bloom's levels for Topic 1</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Test yourself with one difficult integrated case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s27\" title=\"27. Test yourself with one difficult integrated case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Integrated case: EV battery maker</button></li><li data-full=\"28. What you actually need to master\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1n-s28\" title=\"28. What you actually need to master\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>What you actually need to master</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 1 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked examples and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>28 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>This chapter looks deceptively simple because many slides are definitions. The difficult part is that <strong>risk is not one single idea</strong>. The chapter is teaching several different ways to look at the same uncertain event: whether the outcome is known, whether loss is possible, whether the risk can be diversified away, where the risk originates, how severe/frequent it is, and how management should respond.</p>\n<p>The chapter itself covers the definition of risk and uncertainty, the impact of risk on organisations, and the types/dimensions of risk.</p>\n<p>I would build the entire chapter around one mental model:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Objective → Uncertainty → Risk event/exposure → Consequence → Effect on objective → Management response</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Once you understand that chain, most of the apparently unrelated definitions in the slides fall into place.</p>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Start with the deepest idea: risk only makes sense relative to an objective</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_risk_chain\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Every Topic 1 definition sits somewhere on this chain, and the response acts on it.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_objective_lens\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Same event, different objectives</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Move the oil price; the same event helps one firm, hurts another and barely touches a third.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The early slides give the traditional definition:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk is the likelihood or probability of experiencing harm or losing something of value.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In finance, the slides narrow this further to variability in expected return and the chance of financial loss.</p>\n<p>But then the chapter introduces the more powerful definition:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Risk = the effect of uncertainty on objectives.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That second definition is the one you should really understand.</p>\n<p>Suppose a company says:</p>\n<p><strong>Objective:</strong> earn RM100 million profit next year.</p>\n<p>Now imagine oil prices rise.</p>\n<p>Is rising oil price a risk?</p>\n<p>Not by itself.</p>\n<p>For AirAsia, higher fuel prices could negatively affect profit.</p>\n<p>For Petronas, higher crude prices might positively affect revenue.</p>\n<p>For a software company with almost no fuel exposure, the effect might be negligible.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk cannot be evaluated without knowing the objective</span></div>\n<p>That is why the slides specifically say objectives should be clearly defined so that relevant risks can be identified and managed.</p>\n<p>This is a much deeper understanding than memorising:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Risk = possibility of loss.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Risk is really a <strong>relationship</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Uncertainty → Effect on Objective\"><span>Uncertainty</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Effect on Objective</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Risk does not necessarily mean something bad</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_deviation\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Missing the objective in either direction is a deviation management must plan for.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is one of the easiest places to get trapped in an exam.</p>\n<p>The traditional definition focuses on loss.</p>\n<p>But the ISO-style definition in your slide says the effect can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>positive,</li>\n<li>negative,</li>\n<li>or simply a deviation from what was expected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Imagine a company expects:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Sales = RM10m$$</div>\n<p>Possible outcomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM7m,\\ RM10m,\\ RM14m$$</div>\n<p>RM7m is obviously adverse.</p>\n<p>RM14m sounds wonderful, but it is still uncertainty affecting the expected objective.</p>\n<p>Why can a positive deviation still matter to risk management?</p>\n<p>Suppose demand suddenly doubles. The company may now face:</p>\n<p>capacity problems → stock shortages → quality problems → overtime → supplier problems.</p>\n<p>So ERM is not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Find bad things.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Understand uncertainty that may affect achievement of objectives.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That distinction becomes important later when the chapter introduces <strong>speculative risk</strong>, where either gain or loss is possible.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Risk vs uncertainty - understand the information problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_uncertainty_spectrum\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk sits between certainty and uncertainty: the outcomes are known even if the result is not.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slide makes the distinction quite cleanly.</p>\n<p>For <strong>risk</strong>, potential outcomes are known in advance and their odds may also be known.</p>\n<p>For <strong>uncertainty</strong>, even the possible outcomes may not be known, much less their probabilities.</p>\n<p>Think about throwing a die.</p>\n<p>You know the possible outcomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$$</div>\n<p>and approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(X=i)=\\frac{1}{6}$$</div>\n<p>That is risk.</p>\n<p>Now imagine launching an entirely new technology that has never existed before.</p>\n<p>You don't know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>who will use it,</li>\n<li>how governments might regulate it,</li>\n<li>what competitors will do,</li>\n<li>what unexpected applications will emerge,</li>\n<li>what unexpected failures might occur.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is much closer to uncertainty.</p>\n<p>So conceptually:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>Uncertainty</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Possible outcomes</td>\n<td>reasonably identifiable</td>\n<td>may not all be identifiable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Probability</td>\n<td>can sometimes be estimated</td>\n<td>difficult or impossible to estimate reliably</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantification</td>\n<td>more feasible</td>\n<td>much harder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>loan default</td>\n<td>disruptive new technology</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A useful way of thinking about it is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk} = \\text{uncertainty with some structure}$$</div>\n<p>while uncertainty is the broader problem.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Loss exposure: don't confuse the exposure with the actual loss</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides define <strong>loss exposure</strong> as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a situation or circumstance in which a loss is possible, regardless of whether a loss actually occurs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That wording matters.</p>\n<p>Imagine a warehouse worth RM20 million.</p>\n<p>There is a possibility of fire.</p>\n<p>No fire happens this year.</p>\n<p>Was there a loss?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Was there a loss exposure?</p>\n<p>Yes.</p>\n<p>This distinction is essential:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Exposure} \\neq \\text{Event} \\neq \\text{Loss}$$</div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><strong>Exposure:</strong> warehouse containing flammable material</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p><strong>Risk event:</strong> fire</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p><strong>Consequence:</strong> inventory destroyed</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p><strong>Loss:</strong> RM8 million</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p><strong>Objective affected:</strong> profitability/business continuity</p>\n<p>This is how risk managers think.</p>\n<p>They don't wait for the RM8 million loss before saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Ah yes, apparently there was risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would be rather late risk management.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Frequency and severity: the two dimensions behind most risk decisions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_risk_matrix\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Frequency x severity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set how often and how badly a loss could strike; the matrix suggests where management attention and which control belong.</figcaption></figure></div><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_tail_sim\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Same average, different tail</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Two risks with the same RM1m expected loss: simulate 30-year histories and set the capital the firm can lose.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides say risk managers estimate two things for loss exposures:</p>\n<p><strong>Loss frequency</strong> = probable number of losses occurring during some period.</p>\n<p><strong>Loss severity</strong> = probable size of losses.</p>\n<p>This gives us an extremely useful framework.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Low severity</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">High severity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low frequency</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Minor issue</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Catastrophic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High frequency</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Repeated nuisance</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">Serious structural problem</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Consider four examples.</p>\n<p><strong>Paper cuts in an office</strong></p>\n<p>Frequent perhaps, but tiny severity.</p>\n<p><strong>Aircraft crash</strong></p>\n<p>Very low frequency, extremely high severity.</p>\n<p><strong>Shoplifting</strong></p>\n<p>Possibly high frequency, relatively low individual severity.</p>\n<p><strong>Cyberattack that shuts down banking operations</strong></p>\n<p>Potentially lower frequency but massive severity.</p>\n<p>This leads directly into management decisions.</p>\n<p>A useful extension-not explicitly given as a formula in the slides-is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Expected Loss} \\approx \\text{Frequency} \\times \\text{Average Severity}$$</div>\n<p>But don't blindly use that as your only measure.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<h4>Risk A</h4>\n<p>1% chance of RM100 million loss</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.01(100m)=RM1m$$</div>\n<h4>Risk B</h4>\n<p>100% chance of RM1 million loss</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1(1m)=RM1m$$</div>\n<p>Both have an expected loss of RM1 million.</p>\n<p>Are they equivalent?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Risk A could bankrupt a small company.</p>\n<p>This is why serious risk analysis considers <strong>distribution and extreme outcomes</strong>, not merely averages.</p>\n<p>That's already Bloom's <strong>analysis/evaluation</strong> level rather than definition memorisation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Risk identification: management cannot manage something it has not recognised</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_identify_lens\"><div class=\"vis-title\">What each identification method sees</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick an identification method and see which exposures it can and cannot reveal.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides mention several ways to identify exposures, including questionnaires/checklists, physical inspections, flowcharts, financial statements and historical loss data.</p>\n<p>But notice what these methods are really doing.</p>\n<p>They look at risk from different directions.</p>\n<p>A financial statement may reveal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"high debt → financial/liquidity exposure\"><span>high debt</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>financial/liquidity exposure</span></div>\n<p>A physical inspection may reveal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"flammable storage → fire exposure\"><span>flammable storage</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>fire exposure</span></div>\n<p>A flowchart may reveal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"single supplier → supply-chain dependency\"><span>single supplier</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>supply-chain dependency</span></div>\n<p>Historical losses may reveal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"repeated accidents → control weakness\"><span>repeated accidents</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>control weakness</span></div>\n<p>That is why relying on only one identification method is weak.</p>\n<p>A balance sheet will not necessarily tell you that a factory's emergency exit is blocked.</p>\n<p>A physical inspection will not necessarily reveal a looming debt repayment problem.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Risk treatment: avoidance, prevention and reduction are NOT the same thing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_treatment_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Avoidance removes the exposure, prevention cuts frequency, reduction cuts severity.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides divide risk control into:</p>\n<p><strong>Avoidance</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loss prevention</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loss reduction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The easiest way to remember them is through the point in the event chain where they act.</p>\n<h3>Avoidance</h3>\n<p>You remove the exposure completely.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>A company believes operating in Country X is unacceptably dangerous and decides not to enter.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{loss from operating there}) = 0$$</div>\n<p>because the company does not undertake the activity.</p>\n<p>The slide says avoidance means never acquiring the exposure or abandoning an existing exposure.</p>\n<p>But avoidance has a cost:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{No downside} \\quad \\text{but also possibly no upside}$$</div>\n<p>A company that avoids every risky investment will eventually discover the innovative strategy of doing absolutely nothing.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Loss prevention</h3>\n<p>This reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Frequency</span></div>\n<p>The slide's example is installing safety features on hazardous products.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>training workers,</p>\n<p>access controls,</p>\n<p>driver safety programs,</p>\n<p>fraud authentication.</p>\n<p>The goal is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>make the bad event less likely to happen.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h3>Loss reduction</h3>\n<p>This reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Severity</span></div>\n<p>after the event happens.</p>\n<p>The slide gives automatic sprinklers as an example.</p>\n<p>The fire might still happen.</p>\n<p>But instead of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM20m \\text{ loss}$$</div>\n<p>perhaps:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM2m \\text{ loss}$$</div>\n<p>So the exam distinction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Prevention} &amp;\\rightarrow \\text{probability/frequency}\\\\\n\\text{Reduction} &amp;\\rightarrow \\text{impact/severity}\n\\end{aligned}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Now the difficult part: \"types of risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides show several classifications:</p>\n<p>pure,</p>\n<p>speculative,</p>\n<p>diversifiable,</p>\n<p>non-diversifiable,</p>\n<p>systematic,</p>\n<p>non-systematic.</p>\n<p>The mistake would be thinking these are six completely separate boxes.</p>\n<p>They aren't really being classified along one single dimension.</p>\n<p>Think of them as answering different questions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Pure vs speculative risk asks: \"What possible outcomes exist?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_payoff\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Pure risk has no upside; speculative risk spreads across gain, no change and loss.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Pure risk</h3>\n<p>Pure risk gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss or no loss</span></div>\n<p>There is no upside.</p>\n<p>The slides give accidents, illness and natural events as examples.</p>\n<p>Take a car accident.</p>\n<p>Possible states:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\{\\text{accident},\\text{no accident}\\}$$</div>\n<p>You don't make RM50,000 because your car <em>didn't</em> crash.</p>\n<p>Best outcome:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0 \\text{ loss}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Speculative risk</h3>\n<p>Speculative risk gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Gain, no change, or loss</span></div>\n<p>The slide uses stock-market and real-estate investment as examples.</p>\n<p>If you buy shares at RM10:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM15 \\rightarrow gain$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM10 \\rightarrow no gain/loss$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM6 \\rightarrow loss$$</div>\n<p>This is why investing is fundamentally different from something like your house catching fire.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable asks an entirely different question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now stop thinking about gain/loss possibilities.</p>\n<p>The question becomes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Can I make this risk disappear, or substantially shrink it, by holding many different assets?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Diversifiable risk</h3>\n<p>The slides call this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>non-systematic risk,</li>\n<li>particular risk,</li>\n<li>firm-specific risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples include lawsuits, labour strikes, technological innovations and other events that affect particular firms.</p>\n<p>Imagine owning one company.</p>\n<p>Its CEO resigns unexpectedly.</p>\n<p>Your portfolio suffers badly.</p>\n<p>Now imagine owning 100 unrelated companies.</p>\n<p>One CEO resigns.</p>\n<p>Maybe another company's new product succeeds.</p>\n<p>Another gets a favourable court decision.</p>\n<p>The firm-specific events begin cancelling one another out.</p>\n<p>That is diversification.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Why diversification works</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_diversification\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How diversification changes total portfolio risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Add assets and change how alike they are; firm-specific risk fades, the systematic floor stays.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose you own only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A$$</div>\n<p>Your outcome depends heavily on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{market factors} + \\text{A-specific factors}$$</div>\n<p>Now own:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A+B+C+D+\\dots+N$$</div>\n<p>Company-specific surprises are not perfectly correlated.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(+A) + (-B) + (+C) + (-D)$$</div>\n<p>partly cancel each other.</p>\n<p>The slides therefore say that in a large portfolio, unsystematic risk is essentially eliminated through diversification.</p>\n<p>But there is an important condition:</p>\n<h4>The assets must not all react identically.</h4>\n<p>Owning 100 companies all exposed to exactly the same risk is not particularly brilliant diversification.</p>\n<p>Quantity alone is not enough.</p>\n<p>What matters is that the risk drivers are sufficiently different.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Non-diversifiable/systematic risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe non-diversifiable risk as risk affecting the whole economy or broad classes of assets and call it systematic/fundamental risk.</p>\n<p>Examples on the slides include:</p>\n<p>business cycles,</p>\n<p>inflation shocks,</p>\n<p>interest-rate changes,</p>\n<p>productivity shocks,</p>\n<p>major technological change.</p>\n<p>Imagine holding 100 Malaysian equities.</p>\n<p>Bank Negara unexpectedly raises rates dramatically.</p>\n<p>You diversified across companies.</p>\n<p>But every company still exists inside the same economic system.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Diversification cannot remove economy-wide exposure</span></div>\n<p>We can express portfolio risk conceptually as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Total Risk}\n=\n\\text{Systematic Risk}\n+\n\\text{Unsystematic Risk}\n}$$</div>\n<p>As diversification increases:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Unsystematic Risk} \\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Systematic Risk remains}$$</div>\n<p>That is the important insight.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. There is a terminology issue in your slides you should know about</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_systematic_vs_systemic\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Systematic = one common shock to everything. Systemic = failure spreading through linked institutions.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 23 is labelled <strong>Systematic Risk</strong>, but much of its description is actually describing what finance normally calls <strong>systemic risk</strong>: instability spreading through interconnected financial institutions and causing cascading failure. The slide specifically discusses interlinkages, interdependencies and the possibility that one entity's failure causes cascading failure across the system.</p>\n<p>These concepts are related but not identical.</p>\n<h4>Systematic risk</h4>\n<p>Broad market/economic risk.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>inflation, interest rates, recession</span></div>\n<p>Cannot be diversified away easily.</p>\n<h4>Systemic risk</h4>\n<p>Risk that failure spreads through an interconnected system.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Bank A fails</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Bank B suffers losses</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>liquidity freezes</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>other banks fail</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>financial-system crisis.</p>\n<p>For your course, obviously follow your lecturer's terminology when answering from the slides. But conceptually, keep this distinction in your head because otherwise finance terminology becomes unnecessarily messy.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Beta: why investors are compensated for some risks but not others</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_beta\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Beta and market sensitivity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Beta scales an asset&#x27;s typical response to a market move; it measures co-movement, not how wild the stock is on its own.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slide makes an important argument:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unsystematic risk is not \"special\"; systematic risk is the relevant risk for compensation, and beta measures an asset's relative systematic risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Suppose you invest your entire RM100,000 into one terrible company.</p>\n<p>You take enormous firm-specific risk.</p>\n<p>Should the market reward you with a huge expected return simply because you refused to diversify?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>You could have eliminated much of that risk yourself through diversification.</p>\n<p>So finance theory focuses on the risk that investors <strong>cannot cheaply diversify away</strong>.</p>\n<p>That is systematic risk.</p>\n<p>The slide tells you:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta &gt; 1$$</div>\n<p>means more systematic risk than average,</p>\n<p>while:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta &lt; 1$$</div>\n<p>means less systematic risk than average.</p>\n<p>An intuitive interpretation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=1$$</div>\n<p>roughly means the asset has average market sensitivity.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=1.5$$</div>\n<p>means it tends to be more sensitive to broad market movements.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta=0.5$$</div>\n<p>means less sensitivity.</p>\n<p>A useful finance extension beyond your slide is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta_i =\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i,R_m)}\n{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)}$$</div>\n<p>This shows why beta measures <strong>co-movement with the market</strong>, rather than simply how wildly a stock moves by itself.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. A very important higher-order insight: the classifications can overlap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_classify_dims\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>One event, many classifications: name the dimension before naming the risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose a factory catches fire.</p>\n<p>That might be:</p>\n<p><strong>Pure risk</strong> because there is loss/no loss rather than a speculative gain.</p>\n<p>It may also be:</p>\n<p><strong>Diversifiable/firm-specific risk</strong> from an investor's perspective.</p>\n<p>It may simultaneously create:</p>\n<p><strong>Operational risk</strong>, because operations stop.</p>\n<p><strong>Liquidity risk</strong>, if the company cannot meet bills.</p>\n<p><strong>Reputational risk</strong>, if the fire resulted from negligence.</p>\n<p>That is not a contradiction.</p>\n<p>It happens because the classifications ask different questions.</p>\n<p>This is how you move from Bloom's <strong>Remember/Understand</strong> into <strong>Analyse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Instead of asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which single box does this event belong to?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Under which dimension am I classifying it?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Enterprise risk: now move from investment theory to the organisation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_cascade\"><div class=\"vis-title\">A risk cascade</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Step one event through the business: root risk first, then the consequences it triggers.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides define enterprise risks broadly as risks capable of causing monetary/reputational losses or threatening the firm's ability to remain in business. They encompass the major risks facing the organisation, including pure, speculative and financial risks.</p>\n<p>The key insight is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM looks at risks collectively rather than independently</span></div>\n<p>Imagine:</p>\n<p>Cyberattack</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>IT systems unavailable</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Operational risk</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Customers cannot transact</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Business/revenue risk</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Customers complain publicly</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Reputational risk</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Cash inflow falls</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Liquidity risk</p>\n<p>One event can generate a <strong>risk cascade</strong>.</p>\n<p>That's why sophisticated answers shouldn't just say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Cyberattack = operational risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>They should analyse its primary and secondary effects.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Operational risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe operational risk as arising from weaknesses such as internal-control failures, technological failure, mismanagement, human error and inadequate employee training.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Something went wrong in how the organisation operates</span></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>employee enters incorrect payment details,</p>\n<p>software crashes,</p>\n<p>fraud occurs,</p>\n<p>process fails,</p>\n<p>staff are inadequately trained.</p>\n<p>The slides specifically include fraud risk and model risk under operational risk.</p>\n<p>Model risk is particularly interesting.</p>\n<p>Imagine a bank uses:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Credit Model → Approve/Reject Borrower\"><span>Credit Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Approve/Reject Borrower</span></div>\n<p>If the model is flawed:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"bad model → bad decisions → financial losses\"><span>bad model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>bad decisions</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>financial losses</span></div>\n<p>The loss may look like credit risk in the end, but the root cause may be model/operational risk.</p>\n<p>That's a good example of why <strong>root cause and consequence are different</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Liquidity risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_liquidity\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A firm can be rich in assets and still fail to pay tomorrow&#x27;s obligation.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides describe liquidity risk as the possibility that a company cannot fulfil its commitments, potentially due to poor cash-flow management.</p>\n<p>The trap is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Wealth} \\neq \\text{Liquidity}}$$</div>\n<p>A company could own:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100m \\text{ property}$$</div>\n<p>while having:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM50,000 \\text{ cash}$$</div>\n<p>and an RM2 million payment due tomorrow.</p>\n<p>On paper:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Rich company.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In practice:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Good luck paying tomorrow.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Your slides distinguish:</p>\n<h4>Asset liquidity risk</h4>\n<p>Can the asset be bought/sold easily without major difficulty?</p>\n<h4>Funding liquidity risk</h4>\n<p>Can the organisation obtain the cash needed to meet obligations when due?</p>\n<p>That distinction is highly examinable.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Credit risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Credit risk concerns the possibility that a creditor does not receive payment or receives it late.</p>\n<p>The intuitive structure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{I am owed money}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Will the counterparty pay me?</span></div>\n<p>Suppose Bank A lends RM500,000 to Customer B.</p>\n<p>The risk is not that interest rates move.</p>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{B fails to repay})$$</div>\n<p>The slides distinguish retail and wholesale credit risk.</p>\n<p>So always ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Who owes whom money?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That usually reveals credit risk quickly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Market risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_market_moves\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Directional versus volatility risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · The same exchange-rate path seen as directional risk (where it ends) or volatility risk (how much it swings).</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides associate market risk with movements in the prices of financial instruments and distinguish directional and non-directional/volatility risk.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{interest rates} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{share prices} \\downarrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{FX rates change}$$</div>\n<p>A directional position cares about <strong>where</strong> a variable moves.</p>\n<p>For instance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$USD/MYR \\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>might hurt a Malaysian importer.</p>\n<p>Volatility risk cares about <strong>how much the variable fluctuates</strong>, not simply its final direction.</p>\n<p>This becomes very important in derivatives.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Business risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Business risk is the possibility that the company's commercial activities generate inadequate profits because of uncertainty. The slides list consumer preferences, demand, selling prices, input costs, competition, the economic environment and government regulation as drivers.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Can the business model generate sufficient profit?</span></div>\n<p>Suppose Netflix loses subscribers because competitors become more attractive.</p>\n<p>That's fundamentally business/competition risk.</p>\n<p>Suppose its accounting system crashes.</p>\n<p>That's operational risk.</p>\n<p>Same company. Completely different risk source.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Reputational risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe reputational risk as damage arising when stakeholders' expectations are not met and the organisation is perceived negatively. It can arise directly from the firm, indirectly through employees, or tangentially through partners/suppliers.</p>\n<p>A particularly important insight:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Reputational risk is often secondary</span></div>\n<p>For instance:</p>\n<p>Product defect</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{quality risk}$$</div>\n<p>Customer injuries</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{legal exposure}$$</div>\n<p>Social-media outrage</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{reputational risk}$$</div>\n<p>Sales decline</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{business/financial consequence}$$</div>\n<p>So simply saying \"reputation risk\" is often incomplete.</p>\n<p>The better answer asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What caused the reputation damage?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Resource risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Resource risk is the possibility of failing to achieve a goal because necessary resources are unavailable. Resources can include money, skilled workers, time and other inputs.</p>\n<p>Imagine a construction project requiring completion by December.</p>\n<p>Necessary resources:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>money + engineers + machinery + materials + time</span></div>\n<p>If specialist engineers become unavailable:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"resource shortage → delay → cost overrun → objective missed\"><span>resource shortage</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>delay</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>cost overrun</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>objective missed</span></div>\n<p>Again, notice the chapter's original definition reappearing:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"uncertainty → effect on objective\"><span>uncertainty</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>effect on objective</span></div>\n<p>Everything comes back to that.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Now analyse the KFC Malaysia example on page 37</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_category_finder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Ask where the risk originates; the answer names the enterprise category.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The final substantive slide asks what enterprise risk is represented by reports of KFC temporarily closing outlets amid boycott-related economic conditions.</p>\n<p>A weak answer would be:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Reputational risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger Bloom's <strong>analyse</strong> answer separates <strong>cause → risk → consequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Possible chain:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Boycott / changing customer sentiment</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{consumer demand falls}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Business risk</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>lower revenue / outlet viability</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{temporary outlet closures}$$</div>\n<p>There may simultaneously be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Reputational risk</span></div>\n<p>because stakeholder perceptions of the brand influence purchasing behaviour.</p>\n<p>The closure itself creates operational consequences, but saying \"operational risk\" alone would miss the more important underlying driver.</p>\n<p>So I would formulate an exam-quality answer like this:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The incident primarily reflects <strong>business risk</strong>, particularly customer-demand and customer-relationship risk, because changing consumer sentiment and boycott behaviour reduce sales and threaten revenue objectives. It also contains a <strong>reputational-risk component</strong>, because stakeholder perceptions of the brand contribute to the change in demand. Outlet closures are an operational consequence rather than necessarily the original source of the risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is much better than desperately choosing one word from a list.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. The chapter as one integrated system</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The chain runs left to right; the five classification questions each describe one link.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is the model I want you to carry into your exam:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"OBJECTIVE → UNCERTAINTY → EXPOSURE → EVENT → FREQUENCY/SEVERITY → CONSEQUENCE → RISK RESPONSE\"><span>OBJECTIVE</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>UNCERTAINTY</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>EXPOSURE</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>EVENT</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>FREQUENCY/SEVERITY</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>CONSEQUENCE</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>RISK RESPONSE</span></div>\n<p>Then classify the risk using whichever dimension is relevant.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<h4>\"Could there be gain?\"</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Pure vs Speculative}$$</div>\n<h4>\"Can diversification remove it?\"</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Diversifiable vs Non-diversifiable</span></div>\n<h4>\"Where does it originate?\"</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Operational / Credit / Market / Business / Reputation / Liquidity / Resource</span></div>\n<h4>\"How should we manage it?\"</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Avoid / Prevent / Reduce}$$</div>\n<h4>\"How large is it?\"</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Frequency + Severity}$$</div>\n<p>That framework is much more powerful than memorising thirty definitions independently.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Bloom's Taxonomy: how I would expect you to be tested</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_bloom_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Marks rise as the question climbs from recalling a definition to designing a risk plan.</figcaption></figure></div><p>At the <strong>Remember</strong> level:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Define pure risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Easy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{loss or no loss}$$</div>\n<p>At <strong>Understand</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain why an accident is pure rather than speculative risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You explain the outcome structure.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Apply</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A factory installs sprinklers. What technique is used?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Loss reduction, because severity is reduced after the fire occurs.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Analyse</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A company suffers declining demand following negative social-media attention. Identify and explain the risks involved.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you separate:</p>\n<p>reputation → demand → business → financial consequences.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Evaluate</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Should management avoid the market entirely or retain the risk and introduce controls?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now definitions aren't enough. You compare probability, severity, upside, cost of controls and strategic objective.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Create</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Design a risk-management plan for a firm entering a new market.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you'd construct something like:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Objectives → Identify exposures → Classify risks → Assess frequency/severity → Prioritise → Choose treatments → Monitor\"><span>Objectives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Identify exposures</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Classify risks</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Assess frequency/severity</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Prioritise</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Choose treatments</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitor</span></div>\n<p>That is the level of thinking this material should ultimately lead you toward.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Test yourself with one difficult integrated case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_classify_quiz\"><div class=\"vis-title\">One event, every label</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Classify an event on every dimension before revealing the answer.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose <strong>Company X manufactures EV batteries in Malaysia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has only one supplier for a rare mineral. The supplier suddenly shuts down. Production stops for two months. Company X cannot fulfil customer orders, customers complain online, revenue collapses and the company struggles to repay short-term debt.</p>\n<p>Do not answer \"supply risk\" and stop.</p>\n<p>Analyse the chain.</p>\n<h4>First-order risk</h4>\n<p>Supplier unavailable.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Resource/Supplier risk}}$$</div>\n<h4>Second-order risk</h4>\n<p>Factory cannot produce.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Operational consequence}}$$</div>\n<h4>Third-order risk</h4>\n<p>Customers do not receive orders.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Customer/business risk}}$$</div>\n<h4>Fourth-order risk</h4>\n<p>Customers complain publicly.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Reputational risk}}$$</div>\n<h4>Fifth-order risk</h4>\n<p>Revenue falls and debt payments become difficult.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Funding liquidity risk}}$$</div>\n<p>Now evaluate controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Avoidance:</strong> stop using the mineral entirely - probably unrealistic.</p>\n<p><strong>Loss prevention:</strong> diversify suppliers so disruption is less likely to stop production.</p>\n<p><strong>Loss reduction:</strong> maintain safety inventory so production can continue temporarily after disruption.</p>\n<p>That is the kind of reasoning I would aim for in this course.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. What you actually need to master</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Don't study this chapter as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Definition → definition → definition → definition.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Study it as a set of questions:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What objective are we trying to achieve? What uncertainty threatens or changes it? What is the actual exposure? What event could occur? Is the outcome loss-only or gain/loss? Is the risk firm-specific or economy-wide? What is the root cause? What are the secondary consequences? How frequent could it be? How severe could it be? Should we avoid it, reduce its probability, or reduce its impact?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you can answer those questions for an unfamiliar scenario, you understand Topic 1 at the <strong>Apply-Analyse-Evaluate</strong> levels rather than merely knowing the terminology.</p>\n<p>And that distinction matters here, because this chapter is essentially teaching you the <strong>language you will use to reason about every later risk-management topic</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details>");
